Early Detection and Differential Diagnosis of a Progressive Neurodegenerative Disorder Using Wearable Sensor Data and Machine Learning
1st Sridhar D
Alliance School of Advanced Computing Alliance University Bengaluru, India sridhar.devarajan@alliance.edu.in ORCID: 0000-0001-9242-588X
2nd Neeraja Lakshmi K
Alliance School of Advanced Computing Alliance University Bengaluru, India lneerajaPHD26@stu.alliance.edu.in ORCID: 0009-0008-6072-383X
Abstract— Neurodegenerative diseases like Parkinson's disease (PD) tend to only be diagnosed at the end of the neuronal destruction, when there is significant loss. This highlights the importance of low cost, objective, real-time monitoring systems that are able to identify early motor changes. For this, wearable inertial sensors (accelerometers, gyroscopes, and magnetometers) allow real world movement data to be collected without the need for invasive sensors. This work introduces a dual-path system architecture along with a machinelearning pipeline to detect PD using resting tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity, and gait/postural instability. Time domain, frequency domain and statistical features of tri-axial accelerometer and gyroscope signals obtained from a public database containing healthy controls, PD patients and differential-diagnosis cases (essential tremor, ET) were extracted. Two classifiers, SVM and Random Forest and Gradient Boosting were used as classical classifiers and compared with a hybrid CNN–BiLSTM network with temporal attention that was used for stratified 10-fold and leave-one-subject-out validation. Results indicate that wearable-sensor pipelines are evolving for remote screening, and that distinguishing PD from other similar disorders is still difficult, which will be the motivation for multimodal longitudinal wearable monitoring.
Keywords— Parkinson's disease; wearable sensors; inertial measurement unit; machine learning; deep learning; system architecture; gait analysis; early diagnosis; differential diagnosis; smartwatch.
I. INTRODUCTION
Parkinson's disease (PD) is the second most prevalent neurodegenerative disorder, behind Alzheimer's disease, and is one of the fastest growing neurological disorders globally, and is expected to more than double by 2040 due to the aging of the population [4]. PD is associated with a loss of dopaminergic neurons of the SUBSTANTIA NIGRA that presents with a cluster of motor symptoms (resting tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity, gait/postural instability) and non-motor symptoms (sleep disturbance, hyposmia). The assessment of motor signs is subjective, infrequent and is typically conducted in a late phase, largely using scales such as the Unified Parkinson's Disease Rating Scale (UPDRS) and Hoehn–Yahr (H–Y) stage and thus is used as a clinical diagnosis.
The second, under-diagnosed clinical problem, is the differential diagnosis – the differentiation of idiopathic PD from look-alike conditions, which have been gathered under the single term differential diagnosis (DD): essential tremor
(ET), drug-induced Parkinsonism (DIP), atypical parkinsonian syndromes (ATS), vascular Parkinsonism (VP). This is because a misdiagnosis of each condition can have significant implications in the clinical setting, as the prognosis and disease course differ for each condition and even movement disorder specialists do not have greater than a 90% accuracy at initial clinical visit [6].
Inertial sensors such as accelerometers, gyroscopes and, recently, consumer smartwatches are an attractive choice to record out-of-clinic tremor, gait, and postural sway as continuous and objective measurement tools that are ecologically valid [1], [2]. In addition to machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL), wearable-sensor data has been used in (i) screening healthy individuals for the early detection of motor change associated with PD, (ii) tracking the fluctuation and response to treatment, and (iii) aiding in the differential diagnosis of parkinsonian syndromes [4] and [6]. In the systematic review context, the most popular classical classifiers are support vector machines (SVM) and random forest (RF) while recently convolutional neural networks (CNN), long short term memory (LSTM) networks or hybrids of those classifiers are the most popular classifiers and are achieving accuracies ranging from 80% to 95% for binary HC-vs-PD screening on small sets of lab data [3], [10].
This paper has four contributions. First let's quickly review recent data sets and modelling approaches related to the use of wearable sensors for early PD detection and PD vs DD differential diagnosis. Secondly, we propose a dual-path system architecture where we combine a handcrafted-feature classical-ML based branch with a raw-signal CNN–BiLSTM– attention based branch, and present three figures: a block-diagram figure, a network-architecture figure and an experimental-workflow figure for the pipeline. Third, we describe a reproducible implementation of feature engineering and hybrid deep-learning evaluated mainly using the public PADS smartwatch data set [5] one of the very few open data sets that features a differential-diagnosis cohort in addition to PD and HC groups. Fourth, we report and discuss the performance, measured empirically under 10-fold and LOSO cross-validation, under 95.3%/92.7% (10-fold/LOSO) mean accuracy for HC-vs-PD and 90.0%/86.6% (10-fold/LOSO) mean accuracy for PD-vs-DD, putting these into the wider case literature, and justifying the harder nature of the differential-diagnosis task.
