domain features, and gave them to the classical classification algorithms such as SVM, RF and k-NN [1], [13]. A study was performed for the comparison of the performance of 52 classifiers to distinguish PD patients from controls using kinematic features obtained from postural perturbation test during dynamic posturography in 2022, where KNN was the most successful classifier [1]. Results from systematic reviews published from 2009 to 2020 [2] and more recent systematic reviews based on PRISMA guidelines of twelve studies using wearable sensors for diagnostic purposes [3] showed that the majority of studies were only binary classification (HC vs PD or HC vs pre-PD), the most common algorithms used were SVM and RF, and IMUs and accelerometers were the most commonly used sensor modalities.
Since then, bigger multi domain studies have emerged. The WATCH-PD study used a random-forest model derived from cognitive, psychomotor, speech, and mobility data collected from consumer-grade smartwatches and smartphones in PD and age-matched control subjects to achieve 92.3% accuracy, 90.0% sensitivity and 100% specificity (AUC = 0.92) for early-stage PD status [4]. The principal information that is currently available in open data include information on motor fluctuation, response to medication, but not necessarily diagnostic information; e.g., the Levodopa Response Study and the accompanying limb(trunk) accelerometer data set provide information on motor fluctuation and response to medication, not necessarily diagnostic information. Recently, the Wear Gait-PD dataset has been made public using synchronized multi-sensor IMU and instrumented-insole gait data from 100 PD patients and 85 age-matched controls for fine-grained modelling of gait.
Wearable data is not such a well-researched field in differential diagnosis. The PADS (Parkinson's Disease Smartwatch) dataset contains three sets of data: PD from 100 Hz channel samples of the PADs accelerometer and gyroscope taken during a standardized neurological assessment protocol, DD (essential tremor, atypical parkinsonism, secondary parkinsonism and multiple sclerosis) and HC (healthy controls) . Follow-up studies using both tree and deep classifiers of PADS features and adversarial deep networks prove that the task of PADS vs DD is significantly harder than HC vs PD, as there is also significant overlap between the phenotype of tremor and Bradykinesia between the two classes [6] [11]. A related research paper published by MDPI used just one wrist mounted accelerometer and gyroscope for a five-task mobility battery (walk, Romberg stance, Timed-Up-and-Go, cognitive TUG) and focused on the difficulty of distinguishing PD from other parkinsonian syndromes in the clinical environment, highlighting the need for objective tools for differential-diagnosis.
On the modelling side, deep architectures have proven themselves to be more successful than hand-crafted-feature pipelines. CNN–LSTM hybrids have been applied to the raw gyroscope signals and their spectrogram form to achieve the correlation of 0.89 with the clinical rating score of the motor scores in the UPDRS-III on raw gyroscope signals and their spectrogram form [7]. Pure 1-D CNNs on 6-minute-walk-test (6MWT) IMU data have achieved up to 83.5% accuracy for early-stage PD detection, solely using IMU signals [8], and also, recent CNN-GRU-graph (CNN-GRU-graph) ensemble models on gait vertical ground-reaction-forces (vGRF) data
have reported >99% accuracy on gait datasets with PD patients [12] and Bayesian-optimized LSTM/CNN models on vGRF data have reported accuracy >99% and almost perfect AUC for PD-vs-control classification [15]. Small PD datasets have also been successfully used with the aforementioned time-series classifiers ROCKET and Inception Time for the estimation of motor symptoms based on wrist-accelerometer signals [9]. These accuracy ranges (94-96% for HC-vs-PD and 88-92% for PD-vs-DD) are around the upper limit of the accuracy ranges reported in the smaller, single task laboratory gait datasets, and are consistent with the lower, clinically more realistic accuracy ranges reported when a second healthy population was not included, but rather a true differential-diagnosis population. [6], [11].
III.PROPOSED SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE
A. Overall Pipeline
The proposed end-to-end system architecture can be seen in Figure 1. The raw tri-axial acceleration and gyroscope data collected from a wearer's wrist-worn smartwatch is synchronized, filtered and segmented into fixed-length windows. The raw data from the tri-axial accelerometers and gyroscope of the wrist-worn smartwatch are synchronized, filtered and segmented into fixed-length windows. We obtain two parallel model representations from each window: (i) a handcraftedfeature representation, which summarizes each window using time-, frequency-, and statistical-domain features, and pass them to classical machine-learning classifiers (SVM, Random Forest, XGBoost); (ii) a raw-signal representation, which passes the windowed multi-channel time series directly to the proposed deep CNN–BiLSTM network with temporal attention. All the branches are trained and evaluated independently on the same HC / PD / DD cohorts, thus, the deep model's advantage over the classical pipeline is due to the representation learning instead of the differences in the underlying data.
B. CNN–BiLSTM Network with Temporal Attention
The proposed deep-learning branch is explained in detail in Fig. 2. Each window (4 s and 6 channels x/y/z of the accelerometer and x/y/z of the gyroscope) is passed through a series of 3 convolutional blocks of filters (32, 64, 128), followed by batch normalization, ReLU activation and maxpooling to extract local morphological motifs (tremor bursts, gait-cycle transients) without depending on the absolute signal amplitude for each channel of the sensor. The resulting feature-map sequence is given as input to two stacked bidirectional LSTM layers (128 and 64 hidden units) which model longer range temporal relationships in both forward and backward temporal directions over the window. The network is then trained on a temporal-attention layer, which generates a weighting over the time steps that the network uses to focus on the sub-intervals that are most discriminative for
