IV. METHODS
A. Datasets
The main data set is PADS (Physio Net, v1.0.0), which contains a large number (469) of smartwatch accelerometer and gyroscope recordings (Apple Watch series 4, with a sampling frequency of 100 Hz) collected at the University Hospital Minster movement-disorders outpatient clinic between 2018 and 2021 during a structured interactive neurological assessment performed while resting, during postural hold and during movement tasks [5]. We also cite two complementary open gait datasets, the Wear Gait-PD (100 PD, 85 agematched control; synchronized 13-IMU and instrumented-insole gait data) and the multi-domain smartwatch/smartphone dataset for early-stage PD screening (WATCH-PD) [4]. Multiple datasets may be used if reported accuracy bands are to be interpreted in the context of the real world range and not in the context of a single dataset.
B. Preprocessing and Feature Extraction
The raw tri-axial accelerometer and gyroscope data is bandpass filtered between 0.3 and 20 Hz to reduce sensor noise and drift and then split into 4-second windows (with 50% overlapping) in the same format as the input represented in Fig. 2. Time domain (mean, standard deviation, skewness, kurtosis, root-mean-square, signal-magnitude area, zerocrossing rate); Frequency domain (dominant frequency, spectral entropy, power in the tremor band [3-7 Hz] and power in the bradykinesia/gait band [0.5-3 Hz] via FFT); Cross axis correlation features. The features are z-score normalized in each subject to avoid amplitude differences between subjects
and class imbalance (HC, PD, and DD) is addressed using SMOTE oversampling in the folds for training.
C. Classification Tasks and Baselines
Two binary classification tasks were created, HC vs PD (healthy controls vs clinically diagnosed PD patients) and PD vs DD (PD vs other Parkinsonism look-alikes that are not clinically diagnosed PD). Four classical classifiers are trained with the hand-crafted set of features (k-NN, SVM with an RBF kernel, Random Forest and Gradient-Boosted Trees (XGBoost)), while a plain 1-D CNN and the proposed CNN– BiLSTM–attention network (Fig. 2) are both trained directly on the windowed raw signals, which enables a like-for-like comparison between the feature-engineered and representation-learning approaches on an identical train/test partition.
D. Experimental Workflow and Validation Protocol
The experimental workflow and validation protocol are optimized to get the best results. The entire experimental procedure is summarized in Fig. 3 beginning with data acquisition and concluding with the selection of the model. All the models are evaluated by using 10-fold stratified cross validation and leave one subject out (LOSO) cross validation, to avoid optimistic bias in the wearable-PD literature that is frequently found when the same window from the same person is used both in the training and the testing folds [3], [6]. The values given in the tables are mean ± standard deviation of the folds for accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and area under the ROC curve (AUC) separately calculated for 10-fold and LOSO protocols (Table I).
E. Implementation Details and Hyper parameters
The models were built using Tensor Flow/Keras 2.x and trained on one NVIDIA GPU. The CNN–BiLSTM–attention network was trained with the Adam optimizer (learning rate 1e-3, β1=0.9, β2=0.999), a categorical cross-entropy loss, and an L2 weight-decay of 1e-4, for up to 100 epochs with early stopping (patience 10 epochs on validation loss) and a batch size of 32. A dropout layer with 40% dropout rate is used prior to the final fully connected layer. The parameter tuning of classical baselines (k-NN, SVM, Random Forest, XGBoost) was done with grid search on the training partition with 5-fold inner cross-validation (e.g., k ∈ {3,5,7,9} for kNN, C ∈ {0.1,1,10,100} for SVM, 100–500 trees for RF and XGBoost; and RBF γ ∈ {‘scale’,0.01,0.1} for SVM). To prevent information leakage, SMOTE oversampling was used only within the folds of training data and not inside the data folds of validation or test data. All reported results are mean values over 5 random seeds to include variance due to initialization, and the same subject-level train/test partitions were applied to all models for a given fold, so they can be fairly compared. In order to facilitate reproducibility, code and configuration files will be provided after publishing.
