The accuracy of HC-vs-PD is consistently high and is about 5-10 percentage points higher than the accuracy of PD-vs-DD across all models. The proposed CNN–BiLSTM with attention achieves 95.3% ± 1.1 (10-fold) / 92.7% ± 1.7 (LOSO) accuracy (94.6% sensitivity, 94.1% specificity) for HC-vsPD, and 90.0% ± 2.0 (10-fold) / 86.6% ± 2.6 (LOSO) accuracy for PD-vs-DD, outperforming all classical baselines and the plain 1-D CNN on both tasks. Compared to Random Forest, the attention-weighted temporal model scores highest on PD-vs-DD (+7.3 points), indicating that it is best suited to assign each tremor to its correct class. Compared to Random Forest, the attention-weighted temporal model has a higher score on PD-vs-DD (+7.3 points), suggesting it could best distinguish between IDP and ET and atypical parkinsonism, which are only partially separated by handcrafted statistical features.
The HC-vs-PD task has a strong prior: healthy people show very little bradykinesia or gait asymmetry – even very crude amplitude- and frequency-based features have shown good separation between HC and PD, as observed in the WATCHPD study [4] on a similar binary early-PD screening task using the multi domain smartwatch data, and as observed in [1] on a similar binary HC-vs-PD task using postural-perturbation kinematics. The PD-vs-DD task is intrinsically more difficult, because DD is not a single homogeneous class, and each particular class of DD has distinct motor signatures, which can be (e.g., atypical Parkinsonism) either entirely different from those of essential tremor, or overlap with them. The lower absolute accuracy and larger variation across folds compared to HC-vs-PD is due to this heterogeneity and smaller per-subtype sample sizes found in DD cohorts, as has been found for tree-based classification and adversarial-network classifiers in the same PADS cohort [6], [11].
LOSO validation leads to slightly lower accuracy than stratified 10-fold validation for each model, highlighting another point that is consistent with other systematic reviews of the state of wearable PD research: that the limited number of patients in each study, and the short duration of the studies, are restricting the ability to generalize models to individual subjects [3]. The sensitivity for PD-vs-DD is invariably lower than the specificity, with the implication that if a model gives a DD leaning output, this should be a signal to confirmatory clinical evaluation rather than a differential diagnosis in and of itself – this would be a case for a screening deployment.
The results lend credence to two practical conclusions. Wearable-sensor-based screening for early motor signs of PD (HCvs-PD) is currently at a stage of maturity and could be used
