as an additional tool to clinical examination to implement low-cost, population-level screening programs, as suggested by the studies of WATCH-PD and PADS [4], [5], with 94– 96% accuracy using hybrid deep temporal models. Secondly, the accuracy of 90% obtained when using differential diagnosis from motion data only (PD-vs-DD) is still a very challenging problem, at the edge of the 88–92% range reported in similar studies based on PADS data and, for this reason, for a wearable-only system to be used for differential diagnosis it would be still premature.
The current pipeline is limited by the characteristics of the open data used, such as relatively small and demographically narrow DD cohorts, short recordings per single session (vs. at-home monitoring over a longer time period), and device heterogeneity across studies (research-grade IMUs vs. consumer smartwatches), impacting generalization across datasets. Future studies should focus on (i) multimodal fusion of motion data with voice, typing/keystroke dynamics, and non-motor symptom questionnaires, (ii) federated or transferlearning approaches, which enable pooling of data from PD and DD patients across sites without centralization of sensitive clinical data, and (iii) longitudinal at-home monitoring to capture symptom fluctuation and allow for earlier detection, not just at a single time snapshot.
VI. CONCLUSION
Reviewed wearable-sensor data, machine-learning techniques for early detection of PD, in this system architecture with two paths, and built a hybrid CNN–BiLSTM attention pipeline for early detection of PD and primarily tested it on the PADS smartwatch dataset. The accuracy obtained in distinguishing healthy controls from PD patients is 95.3% (10fold) / 92.7% (LOSO), and is similar to and in some cases better than other studies in the literature in the distinction of PD from differential-diagnosis parkinsonism is 90.0% (10fold) / 86.6% (LOSO). The difference between the two tasks suggests that more complex and multimodal and longitudinal data will be required for the robust automated ability to make a differential diagnosis, but that wearable sensors are relatively, if not fully, mature tools for early screening. The wearable-sensor based systems are best suited at this time as scalable pre-clinical screening and monitoring tools for identifying individuals to be referred immediately to a specialist evaluation, rather than for making a clinical diagnosis.
